'''absorbedness''' (ab-sôr′bed-nes), ''n.'' The state of being absorbed, or of having the attention fully occupied.

'''absorbefacient''' (ab-sôr-bē-fā′shie̤nt), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. ''absorbēre'', absorb, + ''facien(t-)s'', ppr. of ''facere'', make.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Causing absorption.

'''II.''' ''n.'' Any substance causing absorption, as of a swelling. ''H. C. Wood'', Therap.

'''absorbency''' (ab-sôr′ben-si), ''n.'' Absorptiveness.

'''absorbent''' (ab-sôr′bent), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. ''absorben(t-)s'', ppr. of ''absorbēre'': see ''absorb''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Absorbing or capable of absorbing; imbibing; swallowing; performing the function of absorption: as, ''absorbent'' vessels; the ''absorbent'' system.
{{smaller block|"Absorption-bands" [in the spectrum] . . . indicate what kind of light has been stopped and extinguished by the ''absorbent'' object. {{right|''A. Daniell'', Prin. of Physics, p. 450.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Absorbent cotton.''' See ''cotton''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Absorbent gland.''' See ''gland''.—'''Absorbent grounds,''' in ''painting'', picture-grounds prepared, either on board or on canvas, so as to have the power of absorbing the redundant oil from the colors, for the sake of quickness in drying, or to increase the brilliancy of the colors.—'''Absorbent-strata water-power,''' a hydraulic device for utilizing the power of water passing through an absorbing-well. See ''absorbing-well'', under ''absorb''.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' Anything which absorbs. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') In ''anat.'' and ''physiol.'', a vessel which imbibes or takes nutritive matters into the system; specifically, in the vertebrates, a lymphatic vessel (which see, under ''lymphatic''). (''b'') In ''therapeutics'': (1) any substance used to absorb a morbid or excessive discharge; (2) an alkali used to neutralize acids in the stomach. (''c'') In ''chem.'': (1) anything that takes up into itself a gas or liquid, as a substance which withdraws moisture from the air; (2) a sub-}}

