'''absorptiometer''' (ab-sôrp-shi-om′e-tėr), ''n.'' [< L. ''absorptio'', absorption, + Gr. μέτρον, a measure: see ''meter''<sup>2</sup>.] An instrument invented by Professor Bunsen to determine the amount of gas absorbed by a unit-volume of a liquid. {{smaller|It is a graduated tube in which a certain quantity of the gas and liquid is agitated over mercury. The amount of absorption is measured on the scale by the height to which the mercury presses up the liquid in the tube.}}

